1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,320
Hi! In this video, we'll look at how you can maintain an authenticated session in Burp.

2
00:00:10,320 --> 00:00:17,280
When testing, some actions may result in an application terminating your session. For example,

3
00:00:17,280 --> 00:00:23,120
an application may automatically log you out if you submit suspicious input. This may prevent you

4
00:00:23,120 --> 00:00:28,720
from performing an action like fuzzing with Burp Intruder. Burp enables you to configure a session

5
00:00:28,720 --> 00:00:34,160
handling rule to automatically log back into an application. The session handling rule determines

6
00:00:34,160 --> 00:00:39,520
whether a session is valid. If it's invalid, it will run a macro to update the session cookies and

7
00:00:39,520 --> 00:00:45,120
log back in. You can follow along with the process below using ginandjuice.shop, our deliberately

8
00:00:45,120 --> 00:00:51,760
vulnerable demonstration site. The process consists of these three steps. Identifying whether a session

9
00:00:51,760 --> 00:00:58,560
is valid or not. Configuring a session handling rule. And finally, checking the session handling rule.

10
00:00:58,720 --> 00:01:06,400
First, we need to identify whether a session is valid or not. In Burp's browser, log into the target

11
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:12,240
website using valid credentials and visit a page that can only be accessed once authenticated.

12
00:01:12,240 --> 00:01:20,720
For example, the My Account page. Then, log out. Now let's see what happens if we try to go to a page

13
00:01:20,720 --> 00:01:27,280
that requires authentication when we are not logged in. If we go to the My Account page, we're redirected

14
00:01:27,280 --> 00:01:34,320
to the login page. If we go to the Proxy HTTP history tab, we can review these requests in more detail

15
00:01:34,880 --> 00:01:39,040
and see that the response to our attempts to access the My Account page when not logged in

16
00:01:39,840 --> 00:01:47,600
results in a 302 redirect to slash login. We can use this as a way of determining whether our session is

17
00:01:47,600 --> 00:01:57,040
still valid. Next, we need to configure our session handling rule. To do this, click on Settings. Under

18
00:01:57,040 --> 00:02:05,920
Sessions, Session Handling Rules, click Add. Go to the Scope tab. Select the tools and URLs that you want the

19
00:02:05,920 --> 00:02:13,360
rule to apply to. In most cases, use the default tool scope and the suite URL scope, which in this case we have

20
00:02:13,360 --> 00:02:19,680
already set to be Gin and Juice Shop. Go to the Details tab and add a unique rule description.

21
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:33,360
Under Rule Actions, click Add, then select Check Session is Valid from the drop-down menu.

22
00:02:34,800 --> 00:02:40,800
Under Inspect Response to determine session validity, we can specify what we see when a user tries to access

23
00:02:40,800 --> 00:02:46,560
an authenticated page when they're not logged in, and specify the aspects of each InScape response

24
00:02:46,560 --> 00:02:53,200
that Burp should inspect for the expression. In this example, we'll set locations to URL of

25
00:02:53,200 --> 00:03:00,720
redirection target, so Burp will inspect the location the 302 redirect sends us to. Under Look for Expression,

26
00:03:00,720 --> 00:03:07,040
we can add Blogin, as this will be specified in the redirect location. For Match Type, select whether the

27
00:03:07,040 --> 00:03:13,760
expression is a literal string or regex. For Case Sensitivity, select whether the expression is

28
00:03:13,760 --> 00:03:19,680
Case Sensitive or Insensitive. And under Match Indicates, select Invalid Session.

29
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:27,280
Under Define Behavior Dependent on Session Validity, select If Session is Invalid,

30
00:03:27,280 --> 00:03:34,880
perform the action below, Run a Macro. Click Add. You should now see the Macro Editor and Macro Recorder

31
00:03:34,880 --> 00:03:43,600
dialog. In the Macro Recorder dialog, select the Login Requests, then click OK. If you're using

32
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:49,600
Gin & Juice Shop, select the Get Login and the two Post Login Requests that send the username and

33
00:03:49,600 --> 00:04:04,000
password details. Keep clicking on OK to close all open dialogs. You should then see the rule is added to

34
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:10,080
the list of Session Handling Rules. Once we've created a Session Handling Rule, it's a good idea to test it

35
00:04:10,080 --> 00:04:17,040
out and make sure it works. So to do this, in Burp's browser, make sure you're logged out of the website.

36
00:04:18,320 --> 00:04:25,120
Then, in the Proxy HTTP History, identify a request for a page that you can only access if you're

37
00:04:25,120 --> 00:04:32,320
authenticated. For example, if you're using Gin & Juice Shop, you can use a Get My Account request.

38
00:04:34,480 --> 00:04:38,640
The request should contain a Session Cookie that is now invalid because we've logged out.

39
00:04:40,480 --> 00:04:43,440
Right-click the request and select Send to Repeater.

40
00:04:45,760 --> 00:04:46,960
Go to the Repeater tab,

41
00:04:46,960 --> 00:04:57,120
and Send the request. Notice that the Session Cookies automatically update. Review the response

42
00:04:57,120 --> 00:05:07,600
and confirm that you've logged in successfully.

43
